Z||000000||8Ball||11-13-2001||07:49 PM||||Ok I have a 86 2m6 My battery went dead so I went to advanced auto to get a new one. They sold me a 75-1 The car still won't start and they swear that is the battery for my car. The battery that came out of it was a 75-5. There is a rather significent differance between the cranking amps in the 2?<P>Are they right is that the right battery?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||JohnF||11-13-2001||08:42 PM||||Everybody understands your concern.  However, some questions need to be answered.<P>Did the starter turn over when you tried to start it?  Did just the solenoid click?  Did nothing happen?<P>Symptoms before and after the battery install, please!<P>||||reg||10
Z||000002||8Ball||11-13-2001||09:00 PM||||Ok my car was running perfect except for the belt constantly squelling. I took the car to Firestone to have the belt fixed ater my 3 failed attempts to tighten it. Turns out advanced sold the the wrong belt.<P>So firestone replaces the belt and that fixes the squeel. But the battery is dead. They jump me off and I get home. The voltage meter was reading just under 13 when I turned the car off. Went to start it again just a click and all the lights went out. When I release the key the lights come back on. So I get the new battery and the voltage meter reads 11 volts??? Same thing turn the key and click and the lights all go out. Did firestone knock something loose or what?<P>Oh yea when advanced tested my old battery it first said good then said the battery was overheated then said it was bad.<p>[This message has been edited by 8Ball (edited 11-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||8Ball||11-13-2001||09:46 PM||||I also checked all my fuses. Everything is in check. Guess I will be late getting to work tomorrow. Gonna try hooking the starter straight to the battery in the morning I guess.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DrDave||11-13-2001||11:09 PM||||     Battery clamps dirty?  Bad ground?  Shure sounds likd a poor connections somewhere, and likly either the positive or negative primary battery wires.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Adrift||11-14-2001||01:10 AM||||I had the same problem on my car - took me days to figure out what it was.<BR>Do this: Get some steal wool/barbecue wire brush (or spend 6 bucks on a "battery terminal cleaning tool" if you use the steal wool or barbecue metal brush make sure they're clean!) and scrub the contacts on the battery/wire until you can see the original color of the metal.  Then attach the wires to the terminals of the battery. Then, using pliers, tighten them some more until they won't turn any more. Should fix your problem.||||reg||1
